WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.750
A keen student practised a

2
00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:05.030
network scan from the course on

3
00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:07.090
the university's live network.

4
00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.550
Within minutes, the security

5
00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.590
team had disabled his account

6
00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:13.270
and called our department.

7
00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:17.170
Today: the ethics of teaching offensive

8
00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:20.270
skills, how to build an isolated lab,

9
00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.670
and how to assess students with

10
00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:24.910
capture-the-flag tasks and write-ups.

11
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.970
The same scan is a lesson in a lab and an

12
00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.010
offence on someone else's network.

13
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.640
Many countries criminalise

14
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.740
unauthorised access.

15
00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.710
So teaching offensive skills requires

16
00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:42.410
written authorisation, a defined scope,

17
00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.670
and an isolated environment.

18
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.680
Before the first offensive lab,

19
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.220
students sign a short code of conduct.

20
00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.790
It names the lab networks they may

21
00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:56.070
test, forbids everything else, explains

22
00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.750
responsible disclosure, and states the

23
00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:00.230
consequences of breaking it.

24
00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.650
A safe lab can run on one server: virtual

25
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.490
machines on a host-only network, no route

26
00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.790
to the internet or the campus, and

27
00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.890
snapshots so any machine can be reset in

28
00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:13.290
seconds.

29
00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.270
In a capture-the-flag task, each

30
00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.150
challenge hides a secret string.

31
00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.440
Finding it proves the technique worked.

32
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:25.390
Flags are easy to auto-grade, but

33
00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.570
they can be shared, so the write-up

34
00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:28.890
carries most of the marks.

35
00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.780
Every attack lab should end with defence.

36
00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.390
After exploiting a flaw, students

37
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.470
write the fix, a detection rule, and

38
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:40.810
a short note for a manager.

39
00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:42.590
That pairing builds

40
00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.730
professionals, not just attackers.

41
00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:46.590
Quick check!

42
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.840
The student scanned the live network.

43
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.200
What should the course have

44
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:53.800
provided to prevent this?

45
00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.570
A signed scope that named the only

46
00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.550
allowed targets, and an isolated lab

47
00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.090
where practising was safe.

48
00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.270
The student needed a place to

49
00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.190
practise and clear limits.

50
00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.640
In the app, open the phishing lab.

51
00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.750
Inspect each message for the signs of

52
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.110
a phish, then discuss how you would

53
00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:17.150
run a phishing exercise ethically,

54
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.430
with consent and no shaming.

55
00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:21.390
Your turn.

56
00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:23.950
Flags from your last exercise

57
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.830
appeared on a public forum.

58
00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:28.250
What change to the assessment

59
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:29.930
protects its validity?

60
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.640
Per-student flags and graded write-ups.

61
00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:37.030
Remember: authorise and scope every

62
00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.110
offensive task, isolate the lab, and

63
00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.850
pair every attack with defence.

64
00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.390
Try it this term: write a one-page code

65
00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:48.210
of conduct for your offensive labs.

66
00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.160
Review it with your university's IT

67
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.680
security team before students sign it.
